(* Quest Self-Hosted Compiler: The SSA's Loops Interface
 *
 * Makes the loops that recursion is visible to the SSA, keeps module cells
 * in registers across them, and moves what is invariant out of them
 * (docs/ssa.md section 4.1).
 *
 * A recursive loop that stays a function, because the IR could not contify
 * it (it is called from more than one place), is a self tail call in the
 * SSA, which only the backends would make a jump. When a function calls
 * itself in tail position once, this pass moves the entry block's
 * instructions and terminator into a new header block whose parameters stand
 * for the function's, and makes the call a jump to the header. A function
 * that calls itself so twice or more is left: its calls are usually loops
 * nested in one function (an outer one inlined into its inner one's), which
 * one header would merge into a shape clang's loop analysis does not take
 * apart. A function from which a stack slot's address may escape keeps its
 * calls, as tail calls would (ssa/passes/tailCalls): a jump would reuse
 * slots that a call would have made anew.
 *
 * A module cell's word that a loop stores to (a top-level tuple's var field,
 * reached through its global's load) is then kept in a stack slot across the
 * loop, which slot promotion makes a register: each predecessor from outside
 * loads it, the loop's accesses use the slot, and each edge that leaves the
 * loop stores it back, a raise's among them. The word is an Int or a Real,
 * read and written only whole; nothing else in the loop can reach it (an
 * access at an overlapping offset through another address); the loop stores
 * nothing to the global, calls only runtime functions that write no memory
 * and cannot raise, and enters no try.
 *
 * Last, what each loop computes the same on every pass moves before it, into
 * a new preheader: pure operations and loads that cannot fault (of a stack
 * slot, of a global the loop stores nothing to, or invariant loads of what a
 * check before the loop found not null). When the loop's header only tests
 * whether to leave, and its first pass then checks invariant values before
 * it stores, calls, or tests what varies (an array's null check, say), the
 * loop is rotated: a copy of the header's test before it, as a guard, leaves
 * where the header would, and otherwise runs those checks, once, then enters
 * the header's successor, so that the header becomes the loop's test at its
 * bottom. The checks raise as the first pass would have, and the conditions
 * pass then decides the loop's own copies of them, which the guard's
 * dominate; the loads they make safe move too. The header's values that
 * other blocks use are kept in stack slots, stored by the header and the
 * guard, which promotion makes block parameters where the two meet. A
 * derived pointer (ptrAdd) moves only out of a loop that calls nothing, so
 * that none is live across a collection without its object's pointer.
 *
 * Then a bounds check (a branch on x <u len to a block that raises) goes
 * when the comparisons before it decide it: x is never negative (a constant,
 * a length, such a value plus a small constant, or a block parameter every
 * jump binds such a value to) and x <= len - 1, by a comparison on a branch
 * that dominates the check or, for a block parameter, on every jump into its
 * block: by the value the jump binds, or, for one that binds x + 1, by a test
 * there that x isnot len - 1 (a for loop's, by induction). Values compare by
 * form (a base and a constant offset, with loads by address and symbols by
 * name), so that len - 1 copied for a guard is the header's; string.length's
 * test for a null string is the length, since under a bound of 0 - 1 no index
 * that is not negative reaches the check. *)

interface Loops
import
    ssa = questlang/ssa/nodes : questlang/ssa/Nodes
export
    (* Each function's, numbering the values again and deciding the
     * conditions again of each that changed. A loop is rotated when its first
     * pass has no invariant check to move before it only if p.rotateAll: it
     * saves the jump back to the test, on arm64; clang rotates loops itself,
     * and a loop rotated before it reaches clang it can compile worse, so
     * ssac's policy turns it off. *)
    loopUnit(u: ssa.Unit p: ssa.Policy): Ok
    (* After ssa/passes/tailJumps: a function's one self tail call, as
     * ssa/passes/tailCalls marks it, a loop, as loopUnit makes one before
     * it, numbering the values of each that changed. tailJumps copies a
     * mutually recursive function into its caller, which can leave a self
     * tail call (even-odd's even1, odd1 copied in, calls even1) that the
     * backends would make a branch to the function's start, passing its
     * arguments again. *)
    loopTailCalls(u: ssa.Unit p: ssa.Policy): Ok
end
